{
  "proposedChange": "DELETE 'genuinely exceptional', leaving: 'We were attracted to Meridian on the strength of a track record that is, in our view, exceptional.'",
  "why": "Removing 'genuinely' tones down the hyperbole while preserving the author’s evaluative view and sentence structure, aligning with the style guidance to reduce marketing language without substituting a different claim or adding new content."
}

{
  "proposedChange": "Halden Group was attracted to Meridian on the strength of a track record that is, in Halden Group’s view, genuinely exceptional.",
  "why": "This keeps the original evaluative language and hedging exactly as written while replacing the first-person references with the named authoring organisation, as required by the style guidance and allowed under the silence rule."
}

{
  "proposedChange": "means key-person risk may be limited",
  "why": "This keeps the original causal framing and claim intact, but adds the modal 'may' to soften the level of certainty, addressing the concern about overreaching causality without changing the substance of the statement in the absence of source data."
}

{
  "proposedChange": "On balance, Halden Group believes the fund should deliver returns broadly in line with its predecessor and Halden Group recommends the commitment.",
  "why": "The only permitted change on a silent card is to replace first-person pronouns with the named authoring organisation while preserving the rest of the sentence. This resolves the voice consistency issue by using third-person institutional voice without altering any claims, hedges, or evaluative language."
}

{
  "proposedChange": "available to Halden Group",
  "why": "The review flags first-person plural as against house style, and the suggestedDirection explicitly permits replacing 'us' with the named authoring organisation. This keeps the original claim and wording intact while aligning with the style rule by making Halden Group the object instead of 'us'."
}

{
